# One row per part that two composed layers are both built from, with the amount that would enter
# twice. The parts the two fusions share, each carrying its own demand and nameplate: if anybody
# adds the two composed layers together, this is the supply counted twice, and this is how much of
# it there is.
#
# In the part's own unit, at its most likely value
#   The figures are the part's, in the part's unit. Each composer may convert the part by its own
#   factor, so in either composed layer's unit the doubled amount is this figure times that
#   composer's factor, and the two need not be equal. The most likely value is enough for what this
#   relation is: a referral that names the overlap, not a sum that owes a range.
#
# The overlap can be worked out here
#   A layer's quantity is a size, and two sizes alone never say how much of them overlaps. Read
#   through the parts, though, the overlap of two composed layers is named: it is a part, a part is
#   a layer, and a layer carries its own figures. So the double count is worked out with nothing
#   filed.
#
# Why `asrt:Elimination` still has to exist
#   Inside one fusion the overlap need not be a whole part: `every-partial-elimination` shares 3 of
#   each part's 10, and `merge-holding-composition/compute` eliminates 90 against parts of 545 and
#   4.4. Nothing in the model names a slice of a part, so those amounts cannot be worked out and
#   must be observed. An overlap inside a part is the only case out of reach, and the elimination
#   is the element that carries it.
#
# Two compositions beside one
#   checks/jagged_layer.sqlc covers one composition and this covers two. That rule follows the
#   paths down from the top of a single composition: a holding composing a group and also naming a
#   member directly is two paths from one top. Two documents composing the same layer is two paths
#   from two tops, no single composition holds both, and widening that rule would change what it
#   asks rather than how far it reaches.
#
# A referral, never a violation
#   Two filings composing the same layer is not a defect in either. It becomes one only if somebody
#   adds the two composed figures together, and whether anybody will is not a question a query can
#   answer. It is the third of a set that all sit on the boundary between filings:
#   entries/spillovers.sqlc finds two layers that move together with nobody saying so, the pairs of
#   layers sharing a unit find two layers with nobody saying whether they are one, and this finds
#   two built from the same supply.
#
# Each pair once
#   The row comparison admits each pair once, in one order, and drops a layer paired with itself,
#   so a composed layer is never reported against itself.
-- composition/parts.sqlc crossed with itself on the part each side names, with that part's own
-- figures.
SELECT a.part_filing, a.part_layer,
       a.composition    AS composition_a, a.composed_layer AS composed_a,
       b.composition    AS composition_b, b.composed_layer AS composed_b,
       (a.composition = b.composition) AS within_one_filing,
       f.d_mode         AS would_double_count_demand,
       f.n_mode         AS would_double_count_nameplate,
       f.d_unit         AS unit
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) a
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) b ON  b.part_filing = a.part_filing
    AND b.part_layer  = a.part_layer
    AND (a.composition, a.composed_layer) < (b.composition, b.composed_layer)
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(layers/figures.sqlc)
) f ON f.filing = a.part_filing AND f.layer = a.part_layer
